<header><p class="enc-kicker">State</p><h1 class="enc-title">Gaochang</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Gaochang was an oasis kingdom near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turfan">Turpan</a> in present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xinjiang">Xinjiang</a>, on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk-road">Silk Road</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tang-dynasty">Tang Dynasty</a> conquered it in 640 during Taizong's campaigns, extending Tang influence into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tarim-basin">Tarim Basin</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/gaochang.jpg" alt="Gaochang" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Colegota · CC BY-SA 2.5 es · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Turpan-gaochang-d10.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Gaochang, also called Khocho or Qocho, was an oasis city on the northern rim of the Taklamakan Desert in present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xinjiang">Xinjiang</a>. One of the earliest cultures of the region was the Iron Age Subeshi culture, from 1100 to 100 BCE, probably linked to the Jushi Kingdom known from Chinese sources. Gaochang itself was built in the 1st century BCE and became a transport hub on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk-road">Silk Road</a>. Under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/han-dynasty">Han dynasty</a> control of the region swayed between the Han and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xiongnu-empire">Xiongnu</a>, until the Jushi leaders pledged allegiance to Han, which set up a military colony and settled colonists from the Hexi region and the central plains.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>After the Western Jin fell, Gaochang was ruled by the Former Liang, Former Qin and Northern Liang as a commandery; the Former Liang ruler Zhang Jun created the Gaochang Commandery in 327. Remnants of the Northern Liang fled there in 439 and held power until the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rouran-khaganate">Rouran Khaganate</a> conquered them in 460. The Rouran appointed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kan-bozhou">Kan Bozhou</a> king, making a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> kingdom. The Gaoche later killed King Kan Shougui and installed vassal kings, and in 501 the people of Gaochang chose Qu Jia as king, founding the Qu kingdom that lasted until 640. The Qu rulers later became vassals of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gokturk-khaganate">Göktürks</a> and were tied to them by marriage. In 629 King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qu-wentai">Qu Wentai</a> welcomed the Tang pilgrim <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xuanzang">Xuanzang</a> with great enthusiasm.</p><p>Fearing Tang expansion, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qu-wentai">Qu Wentai</a> allied with the Western Turks and rebelled against Tang suzerainty. Emperor Taizong sent an army under General <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hou-junji">Hou Junji</a> in 640, and Qu Wentai apparently died of shock at news of its approach. Gaochang was annexed as a sub-prefecture of Xizhou and the seat of government of Anxi. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/an-lushan-rebellion">An Lushan Rebellion</a> the Tang withdrew its soldiers in 755, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibetans">Tibetans</a> and then, in 803, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uyghurs">Uyghurs</a> took the area. After 840 remnants of the Uyghur Khaganate settled there and founded the Kingdom of Qocho in 850, whose people practised <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manichaeism">Manichaeism</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>In 1209 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uyghurs">Uyghur</a> ruler Barchuq offered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genghis-khan">Genghis Khan</a> the suzerainty of his kingdom, and the Uyghurs entered Mongol service as bureaucrats, with their script adapted for Mongolian. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chagatai-khanate">Chagatai Khanate</a> conquered the Gaochang area between 1275 and 1318. The city was destroyed in wars of the 14th century, though palace ruins and the inner and outer city walls can still be seen. Gaochang was inscribed in 2014 on the UNESCO World Heritage List as part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk-road">Silk Roads</a>: the Routes Network of Chang'an-Tianshan Corridor. Nearby lie the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/astana">Astana</a> tombs and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bezeklik-caves">Bezeklik Thousand Buddha Caves</a>, which show depictions of Uyghur sponsors.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Gaochang” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Rouran armies also pushed into the oases, and in 460 they took <b>Gaochang</b> from the last Northern Liang ruler and installed Kan Bozhou as king there.</blockquote><a href="/empire/rouran-khaganate">Rouran Khaganate · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The Eastern Turkic Khaganate was destroyed in 630 and <b>Gaochang</b> conquered in 640, and under Taizong's successor Gaozong the defeat of the Western Turkic Khaganate in 657 extended Tang suzerainty across the Tarim Basin and into Transoxiana, bringing Chinese power to its greatest westward extent.</blockquote><a href="/empire/tang-dynasty">Tang Dynasty · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>In 792 Baoyi Qaghan took <b>Gaochang</b> and destroyed a Tibetan force besieging Yushu; in 808 the Uyghurs seized Liang Province from the Tibetans, though a Tibetan raid of 816 came within two days of Ordu-Baliq.</blockquote><a href="/empire/uyghur-khaganate">Uyghur Khaganate · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>After the Yenisei Kyrgyz sacked the Uyghur capital in Mongolia in 840, the Uyghurs founded the Kingdom of Qocho, with its capital at <b>Gaochang</b> near Turfan, in 866.</blockquote><a href="/culture/tocharians">Tocharians · Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Gaochang</b> grew from a Han military colony in the Turpan basin into the seat of a commandery under Former Liang and its successors.</blockquote><a href="/state/gaochang-kingdom">Gaochang Kingdom</a></li></ul></section>
